// Interaction cases for unify-lens, written in Unify.
//
// Run them with:
//     unify-lens --spec lens/test/spec/interaction.ufy
//
// lens is the front end for this engine and links it in, so a test case
// here is a Unify program. A case names itself, says what is typed, and says
// what must be true afterwards -- and the runner (src/app/spec.cpp) queries
// for all of it, replays each case against the real model, and checks what
// it observes. The vocabulary is closed: an expectation the runner does not
// recognise FAILS the case rather than passing it, so a typo here cannot
// become a green test. Nor can a stray id: a press or expect naming a case
// that does not exist is reported, not dropped.
//
// WHAT BELONGS HERE: anything about the sequence -- what a keystroke does,
// what it must not do, what still holds afterwards.
//
// WHAT DOES NOT: anything the model cannot observe. Colour and layout are
// the golden screens' job (test/golden/), and whether a frame ever reaches
// a terminal belongs to test/pty-interaction-test.cpp, which stays in C++
// because its subject is bytes on a pseudo-terminal -- and because a spec
// runner that needed the terminal to work could not report that the
// terminal is broken.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// TWO SHAPES, AND WHY BOTH
//
// A case can be written FLAT, as a pile of facts tied together by an id and
// a step number, or NESTED, as one term. Both are read; a file may use
// either; this one uses both, on purpose. The history is worth keeping,
// because for a long time the shape was not a matter of taste.
//
// It was flat because nothing else was possible. The obvious shape for this
// file has always been one term per case, and it could not be written:
// LocalSession delivered every solution binding as TEXT -- getSolutionList()
// called toString() on the bound term and the session handed that string
// across the boundary -- even though vault-unify-session.hpp had defined a
// full structured Value (Atom, Int, Cons, Array, Map) for exactly this
// purpose. A nested case would have arrived as the string
// "[step( F1, [panel( Help )] ), ...]" and the front end would have had to
// re-parse the engine's own syntax to read it back: a second parser for one
// language, living in the wrong module. So this file was written flat, and
// said so at length, and named the gap rather than pretending flat was a
// preference.
//
// Engine item E7 closed the gap (plans/todo/lens/E7-STRUCTURED-VALUES.md).
// A binding now crosses the boundary as a tree, and E7.6 added the nested
// reader this file had been asking for.
//
// The flat form was NOT removed, and the mixture below is deliberate:
//
//   - Two readers, with real cases on each, is a suite that defends both.
//     Every case here is a live assertion about lens, so leaving four
//     nested and five flat means a regression in either reader turns this
//     file red on its own -- without anyone having to maintain a fixture
//     whose only job is to be read.
//   - They are honestly good at different things. Nested suits a case that
//     is a SEQUENCE: the order is the whole point, the step numbers are
//     merely positions, and hand-numbering them is a chance to skip one.
//     Flat suits a case that is a handful of independent claims, reads the
//     way a Prolog test suite conventionally does, and lets you append one
//     more `expect` without touching anything around it.
//
// Only the READING differs. Both readers build the same expectation and hand
// it to the same checker (src/app/spec.cpp), so a case means the same thing
// whichever way it is spelled -- and the vocabulary stays closed in both.
//
// TWO NOTATION TRAPS SURVIVE. They are the parser's, they are recorded in
// unify/ROADMAP.md, and both are designed around here rather than papered
// over -- a workaround with no note beside it is how a bug becomes a
// convention:
//
//   - A negative literal in a data position loses its sign: `d( [ -1 ] )` is
//     stored as `d( [( 1 )] )`, silently. So a distance is written with a
//     direction word and never with a sign, in both forms:
//     `moved( cursor, up, 1 )`.
//
//   - An empty list does not parse at all -- `f( [] )` is a parse error,
//     even though findall produces `[]` as a value. So a step with nothing
//     to check cannot write an empty expectation list, and the nested
//     vocabulary carries `step( Keys )` and `repeat( N, Keys )` beside the
//     longer forms for exactly that reason. menu_modal below uses them.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// THE VOCABULARY -- the nested form
//
//     case( Id, Name, Steps )
//     case( Id, Name, Options, Steps )
//
//     Options: a list of
//         layout( browse | run | debug | full )     default: browse
//         geometry( Columns, Rows )                 default: 120 x 40
//
//     Steps: a list of, in order, one step per entry
//         step( Keys )                      type Keys once, check nothing
//         step( Keys, Expectations )        ... and check afterwards
//         repeat( N, Keys )                 N times, check nothing
//         repeat( N, Keys, Expectations )   N times, checked after EACH
//
//     Expectations: a list of
//         visible                  the cursor is on screen
//         panel( Name )            the focused panel
//         cursor( N )  top( N )  row( N )  rows( N )  lines( N )  tiles( N )
//         still( What )            unchanged since the previous key
//         moved( What, up|down, Distance )
//         message( Text )          the status line contains Text
//
// THE VOCABULARY -- the flat form
//
//     case( Id, Name )                  a case, and what to call it
//     layout( Id, browse|run|debug|full )       default: browse
//     geometry( Id, Columns, Rows )             default: 120 x 40
//
//     press( Id, N, Keys )              step N types Keys once
//     press( Id, N, Keys, Times )       ... Times over, checked after EACH
//
//     expect( Id, N, visible )          the cursor is on screen
//     expect( Id, N, panel, Name )      the focused panel
//     expect( Id, N, cursor|top|row|rows|lines|tiles, Number )
//     expect( Id, N, still, What )      unchanged since the previous key
//     expect( Id, N, moved, What, up|down, Distance )
//     expect( Id, N, message, Text )    the status line contains Text
//
// The two do not mix within one case: an id names exactly one case, and a
// flat fact aimed at a nested case is refused rather than merged into it,
// because a nested case already carries its steps in order and there is no
// step number for the loose fact to mean.
//
// Keys are spelled as the keymap spells them: "Down", "C-x 2", "M-x", "F10".


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The bug that started all of this.
//
// Selecting down through help and then reversing: the first Up did nothing
// visible, so the key looked dead and the user pressed it again. Three
// panels each had a copy of `top = cursor >= rows ? cursor-rows+1 : 0`,
// which pins the cursor to the last row and eats the first press back.
//
// Nested: this case IS its sequence. Far enough down that the list is
// definitely scrolled -- the bug could not happen before the viewport
// started moving -- and then ONE Up, after which both the selection and the
// highlight must move exactly one. Before the fix the selection moved and
// the highlight did not.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case( reversal, "reversing direction takes effect on the first press", [
    step( "F1", [ panel( "Help" ), visible ] ),
    repeat( 25, "Down", [ visible ] ),
    step( "Up", [ moved( cursor, up, 1 ), moved( row, up, 1 ), visible ] )
] );


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The same invariant, stated for each panel that scrolls. Three cases on
// purpose: three copies of the scroll arithmetic is how the bug above
// happened, and one case per panel is what proves they now share one rule.
//
// Two are left flat and one is nested -- the split is arbitrary within the
// group and is meant to be: these three are as close to the same case as
// three cases get, so if the two readers ever disagreed about anything, this
// is where it would show as one of three siblings behaving differently.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case( help_scroll, "help scrolls without ever losing the cursor" );
press(  help_scroll, 1, "F1" );
expect( help_scroll, 1, panel, "Help" );
press(  help_scroll, 2, "Down", 40 );
expect( help_scroll, 2, visible );
press(  help_scroll, 3, "Up", 40 );
expect( help_scroll, 3, visible );
// Already at the top: one more Up must be a no-op, not a wrap.
press(  help_scroll, 4, "Up" );
expect( help_scroll, 4, still, cursor );
expect( help_scroll, 4, still, top );

case( menu_scroll, "the menu scrolls without ever losing the cursor" );
geometry( menu_scroll, 80, 24 );        // small, so the list must scroll
press(  menu_scroll, 1, "F10" );
expect( menu_scroll, 1, panel, "Menu" );
press(  menu_scroll, 2, "Down", 30 );
expect( menu_scroll, 2, visible );
press(  menu_scroll, 3, "Up", 30 );
expect( menu_scroll, 3, visible );

// Nested, and carrying the geometry as an option rather than as a fact of
// its own -- so the options list is exercised by a live case and not only by
// a fixture in test/spec/bad/.
case( palette_scroll, "the palette scrolls without ever losing the cursor",
      [ geometry( 80, 24 ) ], [
    step( "M-x", [ panel( "Palette" ) ] ),
    repeat( 20, "Down", [ visible ] ),
    repeat( 20, "Up", [ visible ] )
] );


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Modality. A panel that takes the screen must give it back, and must not
// leak a tile each time -- damage that accumulates over a session is
// exactly what no single-frame golden can see.
//
// The browse layout has four tiles; a modal panel borrows a fifth. Both
// numbers are written down: after five open-and-cancel cycles the counts
// must be the SAME two, which is what "no tile leaked" means. `still` would
// not say this -- it compares against the previous key, and the previous key
// legitimately changed the count.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Nested, and the reason is the middle of it: two of these steps check
// nothing at all, they are only there to run the cycle again. Flat, that is
// a press with no expect beside it and a step number nobody reads; nested it
// is `step( "F10" )`, which is also the one place the empty-list trap shows
// up in a real case rather than in a note.
case( menu_modal, "the menu gives the layout back when cancelled", [
    step( "F10", [ panel( "Menu" ), tiles( 5 ) ] ),
    step( "Esc", [ tiles( 4 ) ] ),
    step( "F10" ),
    step( "Esc" ),
    step( "F10", [ panel( "Menu" ), tiles( 5 ) ] ),
    step( "Esc", [ tiles( 4 ) ] )
] );

case( palette_modal, "the palette gives the layout back when cancelled" );
press(  palette_modal, 1, "M-x" );
expect( palette_modal, 1, panel, "Palette" );
expect( palette_modal, 1, tiles, 5 );
press(  palette_modal, 2, "Esc" );
expect( palette_modal, 2, tiles, 4 );
press(  palette_modal, 3, "M-x" );
press(  palette_modal, 4, "Esc" );
press(  palette_modal, 5, "M-x" );
expect( palette_modal, 5, panel, "Palette" );
expect( palette_modal, 5, tiles, 5 );
press(  palette_modal, 6, "Esc" );
expect( palette_modal, 6, tiles, 4 );


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tiling. `C-x 2` is two keys, and the state between them -- the pending
// chord -- is a state a user can see and get stuck in.
//
// Flat, both of them, and this is the shape's own argument: each line is one
// independent claim about one key, and nothing about either case is a
// sequence beyond the fact that keys happen in order.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case( tiling, "splitting adds exactly one tile, closing removes it" );
press(  tiling, 1, "C-x 2" );
expect( tiling, 1, moved, tiles, down, 1 );
press(  tiling, 2, "C-x 3" );
expect( tiling, 2, moved, tiles, down, 1 );
press(  tiling, 3, "C-x 0" );
expect( tiling, 3, moved, tiles, up, 1 );
press(  tiling, 4, "C-x 0" );
expect( tiling, 4, moved, tiles, up, 1 );

case( chord, "an unfinished chord changes nothing" );
press(  chord, 1, "C-x" );
expect( chord, 1, still, tiles );
press(  chord, 2, "C-g" );
expect( chord, 2, still, tiles );


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// A stock layout other than the default, to prove the option is honoured
// rather than quietly ignored. Nested, so that the options list is read for
// `layout` as well as for `geometry` -- an option the reader accepted but
// then dropped would look exactly like a passing case, which is why the
// layout it asks for is one the default would not give it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case( run_layout, "help opens over the run layout too", [ layout( run ) ], [
    step( "F1", [ panel( "Help" ), visible ] ),
    repeat( 10, "Down", [ visible ] )
] );
